A||||1||mrfixit58||How to Repair your Blown Sub-woofer Speaker||||6||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||09-05-2000||09:31 AM||||As I mentioned in an earlier thread, I got a price of $50 to re-foam my speaker or $38 for a replacement from Rodney. Well, this weekend I got tired of seeing my sub-woofer wires hanging down on the passenger floor so I decided to buy a cheap 5 1/4 speaker for a temporary replacement. <P>After I modified the mounting holes in the new speaker I mounted it in the sub-wooker box and gave it a try. It sounded like CRAP! I don't know if it was because the coil was too small or the resistance was wrong but I knew it wouldn't work. So I took it out and set it on the bench and wondered what I was going to do with another speaker shaped paperweight when it stuck me. Why not try to use the new speaker to repair the old? So, that's what I did and now it sounds GREAT. It only took about one hour for all the work.<BR>If you want to try it, this is how:<P>1) Take a small screwdriver and pry loose the ring and fabric grill that covers the speaker.<P>2) remove all old foam from the speaker cone and the metal frame.<P>3) Take a good 5 1/4 speaker ($9.50 from Kmart) and, using a razer, cut loose the outer edge of the foam ring. Try to get as much material as possible.<P>4) Cut the cone (new speaker) to remove the foam ring. Cut is as close to the voice coil as possible because it will be trimmed once removed.<P>5) Trim off the excess cone material (new speaker) but leave enough to overlap the cone on the old speaker.<P>6) Using a small hobby paint brush, apply rubber cement around the outer edge of the old speaker cone (front side) and around the inner edge (back side) of the new foam ring. <P>7) Center the foam ring and press onto the old speaker cone. Let dry.<P>8) Using the brush and rubber cement, glue the outer edge of the foam ring to the speaker frame.<P>9) Place the original speaker grill cover on the speaker, turn the speaker upside down and palce a weight on top and let dry.<P>10) Re-install speaker and enjoy!<P>Since I had the rubber cement and the brush, it cost me less than $10 to fix the speaker. If you can find someone to sell you just the foam ring, it may cost less. <P>Hope this helps someone,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
